-- VECTRIC LUA SCRIPT
-- =====================================================]]
-- Shaker Maker help page. Loaded on demand by OnLuaButton_InquiryHelpMain in Shaker_Maker.lua, as Hold Down Helper
-- loads its own, and shown in an HTML_Dialog. ButtonOK is the dialog's Close button.
-- =====================================================]]
function ShakerHelpHtml()
    return [==[<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><title>Shaker Maker Help</title>
<style>
body { font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 13px; background-color: #FFFFFF; color: #000000; margin: 0; }
.layout { overflow: auto; height: 540px; padding: 4px 14px 4px 10px; }
h1 { font-size: 20px; margin: 10px 0 4px 0; }
h2 { font-size: 16px; margin: 16px 0 4px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #999999; }
p, li { line-height: 1.35; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 4px 0; }
th, td { border: 1px solid #BBBBBB; padding: 3px 6px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
th { background-color: #EEEEEE; }
.warn { background-color: #FFF4CE; border: 1px solid #D9A400; padding: 6px 8px; margin: 8px 0; }
.foot { text-align: center; padding: 8px; border-top: 1px solid #999999; }
.FormButton { font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; }
</style></head>
<body>
<div class="layout">

<h1>Shaker Maker</h1>
<p>Carves a shaker look into a solid slab, such as MDF: the center panel is recessed, and the rails and stiles are
left standing as a raised frame around it. Each run draws the parts and creates their toolpaths.</p>

<h2>Part</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Name, Quantity</td><td>Each part is labeled with the name (numbered when there is more than one) and its
size. Identical parts are laid out side by side.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Width (X), Length (Y)</td><td>Overall size of the door or drawer front.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thickness</td><td>Thickness of the slab. Parts go on a sheet named for it, created if needed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recess Depth</td><td>How far the panel sits below the face of the frame.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stile Width, Rail Width</td><td>Width of the left/right and top/bottom frame members.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Panel Edge</h2>
<p><b>Straight</b> cuts a square wall. The clearing bits cut it, so the panel's corners are left round, at the
radius of the smallest clearing bit in use. The completion message states that radius.</p>
<p><b>V-Bit</b> cuts a beveled wall with sharp corners. The bevel is <i>depth &times; tan(V-bit angle / 2)</i>
wide: 0.25 at a depth of 0.25 with a 90&deg; bit. <b>Measure Widths To</b> says where the rail and stile widths end:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Bottom of Bevel</b>: the frame, bevel included, is the width you entered. The flat face of the frame is
narrower by the bevel width.</li>
<li><b>Top of Bevel</b>: the flat face of the frame is the width you entered, and the bevel comes out of the
panel.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bits</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Bit</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bulk Clearing Bit</td><td>Clears the panel floor. Use the largest bit the floor allows, such as a large
carbide insert cutter: it must be smaller than the floor's shorter side.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corner Bit 1, 2</td><td>Optional. Each cleans up the corners the bit before it could not reach, by pocketing
a small square in each floor corner. Each must be smaller than the one before.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V-Bit</td><td>V-Bit edge only. Cuts the bevel. Its diameter must be at least twice the bevel width to reach
the recess depth.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Profile Bit</td><td>Cuts each part out of the sheet when <b>Cut Out Parts</b> is ticked. Untick it when the
slab is already cut to size; the part outline is still drawn, and shows where the slab must sit.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Toolpaths</h2>
<p>Toolpaths are associated with the layers they cut, so a later run with the same thickness and recess depth
recalculates them to include its parts rather than adding duplicates. They are ordered to cut the floor first,
then the corners from the largest bit to the smallest, then the V-bit bevel, then the cut-out.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Toolpath</th><th>Layer</th></tr>
<tr><td>SM Pocket <i>depth</i></td><td>SM Pocket (<i>thickness</i>, <i>depth</i>): the floor, for a straight edge</td></tr>
<tr><td>SM V-Carve <i>depth</i></td><td>SM V-Carve (<i>thickness</i>, <i>depth</i>): the top of the bevel, for a V-bit edge.
The bulk bit clears its flat floor.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SM Corners 1/2 <i>depth</i></td><td>SM Corners 1/2 (<i>thickness</i>, <i>depth</i>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>SM Profile</td><td>SM Profile (<i>thickness</i>)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="warn"><b>A reused toolpath keeps the bits it was created with.</b> If a later run with the same
thickness and depth chooses different bits, edit the existing toolpaths or delete them before running.</div>

</div>
<div class="foot"><input id="ButtonOK" class="FormButton" type="button" value="Close"></div>
</body></html>]==]
end
-- =============== End of File =========================]]
